.TH MU2EDAQ-RUNLOCK 1 "2026-09-29" "mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.2.0" "Mu2e DAQ Operations"
.SH NAME
mu2edaq-runlock \- report on the power-recovery run lock
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B python \-m mu2edaq_power_recovery.runlock
.RB { status | pid }
.RB [ \-\-lock\-file
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-env\-file
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Every
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1)
invocation that can act on hardware (phases 1\(en3 without
.BR \-\-simulate )
holds an exclusive operating-system lock on
.I run.lock_file
for its lifetime
.RB ( flock (2)
on Linux and macOS,
.B msvcrt.locking
on Windows) and writes a JSON record
.RI { pid ", " started_at ", " cmdline ", " host }
into the file. This helper reads that state. It never signals, deletes or
rewrites anything.
.PP
The record is believed only while the lock is held. The kernel releases the
lock when the holder exits, however it exits, so a free lock with a record in
the file means the recorded pid is stale \(em possibly already reused by an
unrelated process \(em and it is not reported as a pid.
.PP
To find out whether the lock is free the helper takes it for an instant and
releases it; a run starting in that instant would exit 2 as if busy.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Print
.I "running: pid N on host H, started T, command: ..."
or
.IR "not running (stale record: ...)" .
.TP
.B pid
Print the holder's pid and nothing else, only while the lock is held. This is
what the stop scripts signal, after checking the process's command line.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-lock\-file " PATH"
Lock file. Default:
.I run.lock_file
from the layered configuration, normally
.IR logs/power-recovery.lock .
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE" "\fR, \fP" \-\-env\-file " FILE"
Configuration used to find
.IR run.lock_file .
.TP
.B \-\-json
.BR status :
one object
.RI { running ", " lock_file ", " holder ", " stale_record }.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
.BR status :
a run holds the lock.
.BR pid :
the pid was printed.
.TP
.B 1
The lock is free (any recorded pid is stale), or for
.BR pid ,
it is held from another host and cannot be signalled from here.
.TP
.B 2
The lock could not be examined, or it is held with no readable record.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.EX
python \-m mu2edaq_power_recovery.runlock status
python \-m mu2edaq_power_recovery.runlock pid \-\-lock\-file /tmp/test.lock
\&./stop\-mu2edaq\-power\-recovery.sh \-\-status
.EE
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I logs/power-recovery.lock
The default lock file.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1),
.BR mu2edaq-power-recovery-bootstrap (1),
.BR mu2edaq-power-recovery.yaml (5)
.SH AUTHOR
A. Norman <anorman@fnal.gov>, Mu2e DAQ Operations.
